WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.635
This week, we're going to be talking about

2
00:00:08.635 --> 00:00:13.215
two seemingly different ideas: procrastination and memory.

3
00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:17.020
But the two topics are intimately related, why?

4
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.835
Because building solid chunks in long-term memory,

5
00:00:20.835 --> 00:00:25.645
chunks that are easily accessible by your short term memory takes time.

6
00:00:25.645 --> 00:00:29.890
It's not the kind of thing you want to be putting off until the last minute.

7
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:33.370
You already have one good tool for procrastination, the pomodoro,

8
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:38.410
that powerful 25 minute concentrated period of energized focus.

9
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.010
This week, we're going to fill you in with more information about how

10
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.925
procrastination happens and simple ways to tackle it,

11
00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:47.360
and this is key,

12
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.340
ways that don't take much will power.

13
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.770
Then we'll move on to talking about some of the best ways to access

14
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.585
your brain's most powerful long-term memory systems.

15
00:00:57.585 --> 00:01:02.260
I'm Barbara Oakley, thanks for learning how to learn.